1
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:13,720
All right. Let's jump
into Socket Programming.

2
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:17,580
Stepping back, remember,

3
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:20,855
the transport layer provides

4
00:00:20,855 --> 00:00:26,025
the ability to perform process
to process communication.

5
00:00:26,025 --> 00:00:27,825
Therefore using TCP,

6
00:00:27,825 --> 00:00:33,280
it provides this reliable
in order stream of data.

7
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:37,840
How the transport layer
provides this process

8
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:42,210
to process communication is
through an extra header,

9
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:46,960
and the fields that
are addressing ports.

10
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:49,920
This allows the
operating system,

11
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:52,760
when it gets a datagram,

12
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:55,740
it can know what application or

13
00:00:55,740 --> 00:01:00,215
what process this
particular data is for.

14
00:01:00,215 --> 00:01:07,080
It's in this picture showing
it directing it to port 80.

15
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,220
The key problem that
we're going to look at

16
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:16,360
here is how to
actually program this.

17
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:18,380
The link network and

18
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:22,025
transport layer protocols
are just concepts,

19
00:01:22,025 --> 00:01:24,760
and it's the operating
system network stack

20
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:27,640
that is their implementation.

21
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:29,980
How do we write programs to

22
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:32,960
interface to this network stack?

23
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:40,030
The concept here or the
answer is Berkeley Sockets.

24
00:01:40,030 --> 00:01:43,230
This originated in 1983,

25
00:01:43,230 --> 00:01:45,970
and has been the standard since.

26
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:50,200
A socket is an abstract
representation

27
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:54,020
for the local endpoint of a
network communication path.

28
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:58,275
One application can put
data into a socket,

29
00:01:58,275 --> 00:02:00,140
and that would enable

30
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:05,095
the other application to
get data from the socket.

31
00:02:05,095 --> 00:02:08,800
Through everything
under that interface

32
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:10,660
is the operating system and

33
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:12,620
all these different
protocols that are

34
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:16,480
happening to make
that transfer occur.

35
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:23,560
This is a diagram of socket
programming in general.

36
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:25,700
Each of these is
a function call,

37
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:27,860
and this is the general order

38
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:30,280
that you would
have to call them.

39
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:36,000
If you haven't heard the term
client and server before,

40
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,220
client represents the one

41
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:42,255
that's going to
initiate a connection.

42
00:02:42,255 --> 00:02:47,560
A server is the one that's
going to accept connections,

43
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:51,020
and then after that they're
able to communicate.

44
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:55,040
We saw this with the TCP
three way handshake.

45
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:57,880
The one that's initiating
the connection,

46
00:02:57,880 --> 00:02:59,600
we're going to call a client,

47
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:03,305
and the other one we're
going to call the server.

48
00:03:03,305 --> 00:03:07,300
Now we're going to go through
this diagram piece by

49
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:12,815
piece in the remaining
part of this video.

50
00:03:12,815 --> 00:03:18,845
Let's start at the
top, the TCP server.

51
00:03:18,845 --> 00:03:25,370
This also largely
the same for UDP.

52
00:03:25,370 --> 00:03:28,930
Slightly different
functions that you'll call

53
00:03:28,930 --> 00:03:32,190
because it's not the exact same,

54
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:36,780
but this does refer to both.

55
00:03:36,780 --> 00:03:39,165
You would open up a socket,

56
00:03:39,165 --> 00:03:44,540
and here the OS will create a
socket and return a handle.

57
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:49,120
It's a file descriptor.

58
00:03:49,290 --> 00:03:52,090
If you're programming in C,

59
00:03:52,090 --> 00:03:55,015
it would look
something like this,

60
00:03:55,015 --> 00:03:58,435
where you specify whether it's

61
00:03:58,435 --> 00:04:05,685
an IPV4 address or an IPV6
address that you want.

62
00:04:05,685 --> 00:04:10,310
The type is going to be TCP,

63
00:04:10,310 --> 00:04:14,530
that's a stream or UDP,

64
00:04:14,530 --> 00:04:18,250
just the datagram itself.

65
00:04:19,130 --> 00:04:21,510
Once you've created that socket,

66
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:24,255
it tells the Operating System

67
00:04:24,255 --> 00:04:28,510
creates some state
representing this connection.

68
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:32,775
Then you would bind it,

69
00:04:32,775 --> 00:04:34,290
so you call bind.

70
00:04:34,290 --> 00:04:39,940
This tells the Operating
System address to use.

71
00:04:40,890 --> 00:04:44,650
Again, if you're
using C here there's

72
00:04:44,650 --> 00:04:48,750
a field called my adder,

73
00:04:48,750 --> 00:04:52,030
and this is the IP
address and port number.

74
00:04:52,030 --> 00:04:54,650
If you're running a web server,

75
00:04:54,650 --> 00:04:57,070
you would bind it to port 80.

76
00:04:57,070 --> 00:04:59,890
If you're running
the SSH server,

77
00:04:59,890 --> 00:05:02,550
you would bind it to port 22.

78
00:05:02,550 --> 00:05:06,030
If you have multiple
IP addresses,

79
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:09,670
for example have multiple
network interfaces, well,

80
00:05:09,670 --> 00:05:12,150
you can choose an IP address,

81
00:05:12,150 --> 00:05:16,170
or you can bind it
to the local host,

82
00:05:16,170 --> 00:05:19,530
or all of them.

83
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:23,760
The next thing that
this server would

84
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:28,670
do would be to listen
for connections.

85
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:32,670
Listen notifies the
operating system of

86
00:05:32,670 --> 00:05:34,650
the willingness to accept

87
00:05:34,650 --> 00:05:38,245
incoming connections
on this socket.

88
00:05:38,245 --> 00:05:43,360
Then would get called.

89
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:46,090
This is where the server

90
00:05:46,090 --> 00:05:49,930
would block waiting
for the connections.

91
00:05:49,930 --> 00:05:56,520
It sets the address of the
incoming connection that

92
00:05:59,170 --> 00:06:03,575
This is the address
of the client,

93
00:06:03,575 --> 00:06:10,685
would be effectively the return
value of calling accept.

94
00:06:10,685 --> 00:06:12,950
At this point, the
server is ready,

95
00:06:12,950 --> 00:06:16,280
it's willing, it's
waiting for connections.

96
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:21,605
Now a client can
initiate the connection.

97
00:06:21,605 --> 00:06:24,890
It would also call socket to

98
00:06:24,890 --> 00:06:27,920
create the state in
the operating system.

99
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:31,275
Get a file descriptor to be
able to refer to this socket,

100
00:06:31,275 --> 00:06:33,775
and then it will call connect

101
00:06:33,775 --> 00:06:36,790
to do the connection
establishment.

102
00:06:36,790 --> 00:06:42,780
This would be something
that's unique to TCP.

103
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:47,720
Then sense that first of
the three way handshake,

104
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:52,265
the operating system
performs that handshake

105
00:06:52,265 --> 00:06:55,385
and at that point

106
00:06:55,385 --> 00:06:59,195
the accept would then
become unblocked and say,

107
00:06:59,195 --> 00:07:01,580
great, I have a new connection.

108
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:05,300
Connect on the client side
tells the operating system to

109
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:08,855
initiate the connection
to a specific address.

110
00:07:08,855 --> 00:07:14,270
Here you would specify the
address of the server,

111
00:07:14,270 --> 00:07:18,680
the IP address and port number.

112
00:07:19,410 --> 00:07:23,510
Then at that point, there's now

113
00:07:23,510 --> 00:07:27,830
this exchange of read
and write calls,

114
00:07:27,830 --> 00:07:33,320
or in this case it
says send and receive.

115
00:07:33,320 --> 00:07:35,930
Each of these is
going to match up

116
00:07:35,930 --> 00:07:39,845
whenever you're expecting
to receive something.

117
00:07:39,845 --> 00:07:42,140
You just wait there for

118
00:07:42,140 --> 00:07:45,515
the data and the
client would send it.

119
00:07:45,515 --> 00:07:50,825
This just goes on and on
forever or for as long

120
00:07:50,825 --> 00:07:56,510
as the application
layer protocol,

121
00:07:56,510 --> 00:08:00,350
which would dictate how

122
00:08:00,350 --> 00:08:02,900
this data is structured
and how you would

123
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:06,690
know when to send and receive.

124
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:10,940
Now if the client ever

125
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:16,130
finishes and calls
the close function,

126
00:08:16,130 --> 00:08:19,370
which is like closing a file,

127
00:08:19,370 --> 00:08:21,785
it's closing the socket.

128
00:08:21,785 --> 00:08:24,095
This would actually send

129
00:08:24,095 --> 00:08:28,160
a TCP message closing
the connection,

130
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:30,740
it would say this connection is

131
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:36,420
finished when the server
is waiting to receive.

132
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:38,140
That would actually respond with

133
00:08:38,140 --> 00:08:43,495
a special return values
such that it would know,

134
00:08:43,495 --> 00:08:47,150
I should close my
socket as well.

135
00:08:48,130 --> 00:08:54,440
That's the entirety of
socket programming.

136
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:57,980
Now to see it as an
example in Python,

